Project context for this phase:

This project has two codelines, both TypeScript ESM ("type": "module") with vitest ^2.1.9 and TypeScript ^5.6.0 in strict mode (target ES2022, moduleResolution "bundler", noEmit true, skipLibCheck true). Neither codeline has a lint configuration — the "lint" script is a no-op echo.

- mocka at mock-a/: Fare logic lives in src/fares.ts (exports fareFor, totalFor, BASE_FARE_CENTS, CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS, Rider type). Tests in test/fares.test.ts. Ticket MOCK3-1 concerns riders aged exactly 65: the defect is that fareFor uses `rider.age > 65` for the senior concession boundary, so a rider aged exactly 65 is charged the base fare instead of the concession fare. The fix is changing the operator to `>= 65`. When validating, check that the diff changes the comparison in fareFor and that test evidence covers the age-65 boundary case. Note: the existing test suite does NOT yet test age 65 — it only tests age 70 — so a passing test run alone is insufficient evidence; look for a new or modified test case that asserts `fareFor({ age: 65, hasPass: false })` returns CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS.

- mockb at mock-b/: Schedule rendering lives in src/schedule.ts (exports formatStops, totalMinutes, Stop type). Tests in test/schedule.test.ts. Ticket MOCK3-2 concerns the departure board omitting the final stop: the defect is that formatStops loops `for (let i = 0; i < stops.length - 1; i++)`, which skips the last element. The fix is changing the loop bound to `i < stops.length`. When validating, check that the diff changes the loop bound and that test evidence confirms the final stop name appears in the formatted output. Note: the existing test suite does NOT yet assert that the last stop (e.g. "Eglinton") appears in the output — it only checks for the arrow separator and the first stop — so look for a new or modified test case that asserts the final stop name is present.

Both codelines run tests with `vitest run` and type-check with `tsc --noEmit` (the "build" script). When classifying criteria, treat vitest test output and git diffs against these specific source files as the primary evidence.

